<p>Wes Streeting, who resigned as Britain’s health secretary last week, said he would stand in any Labour leadership contest to replace Prime Minister Keir Starmer and called Britain’s 2016 vote to leave the European Union “a catastrophic mistake”. Speaking at a Progress conference in London, Streeting presented a pro-European platform and said Britain should seek membership again one day. Streeting has not formally triggered a leadership contest. He argued Labour needed an open race and warned that moving too quickly, before Greater Manchester Mayor Andy Burnham had a chance to return to Parliament, could extend instability. Burnham, Culture Secretary Lisa Nandy and Conservative Party chairman Kevin Hollinrake responded to the speech. Starmer has rejected calls to step down after Labour suffered heavy local election defeats last week.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/9iZRw7Ct-UB5aEvUx86LOGmaM7EHtlck8wywyJi5pjP-2RHCd_QShArfUXHJ4vpqtJo8MbNQ4cVectCyNsxU3Ga3gGY42_TrbJjnvv4Ynpa2liKwXO1Z9O21LvJiLVQatdoA2YUAGgWCJi2HjbE1dr3QPMvON2QERjNWUQF56KDoxEBdWhqBTHJMHRQbypjjMjVZ7UX2hzttEbfMrHA4o1cp9bCiABqj_Q1_ibhvrAYqaz9FM9WtWDXsJJsbBRx4aVYThSyeeQy04uGaCilARMmlJv2c' alt='Wes Streeting speaking at the Progress Conference at Convene in London' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Policy split: Starmer opposed Brexit but, as prime minister, has ruled out rejoining the EU or making major concessions on issues such as immigration that Brussels considers central to closer economic ties.</li><li>By-election stakes: Channel 4 reported the Makerfield contest was likely to be held around June 18 and would test whether Burnham could defeat Reform in a seat where it had won heavily the previous week.</li><li>Fiscal pledge: The Financial Times reported that Burnham committed to Rachel Reeves’ fiscal rules as part of an effort to reassure markets.</li></ul><blockquote>We need a new special relationship with the EU, because Britain’s future lies with Europe, and one day — one day — back in the European Union - Wes Streeting</blockquote><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Wes Streeting: Streeting said he hoped any Labour leadership race would be “good-natured” and “focused on ideas,” casting the contest as a debate over Labour’s direction rather than a purely personal fight. (<a href='https://www.theguardian.com/politics/video/2026/may/16/wes-streeting-labour-leadership-contest-andy-burnham-keir-starmer-video'>The Guardian</a>)</li><li>Andy Burnham: Burnham said he saw a long-term case for rejoining the EU but would not make that argument during the by-election campaign, saying Britain should focus on domestic issues affecting people. (<a href='https://www.huffingtonpost.co.uk/entry/streeting-throws-down-brexit-gauntlet-to-burnham-as-labour-splits-erupt_uk_6a096647e4b0ee71697307a1'>HuffPost UK</a>)</li><li>Lisa Nandy: Nandy challenged Streeting’s remarks on EU re-entry, calling them “odd” as Labour figures argued over whether Britain should try to rejoin the bloc. (<a href='https://www.theguardian.com/politics/2026/may/17/culture-secretary-lisa-nandy-says-wes-streeting-rejoin-eu-call-odd'>The Guardian</a>)</li><li>Kevin Hollinrake: The Conservative Party chairman said Labour’s Brexit argument was a distraction from the cost of living, the economy, public services and defence. (<a href='https://www.huffingtonpost.co.uk/entry/streeting-throws-down-brexit-gauntlet-to-burnham-as-labour-splits-erupt_uk_6a096647e4b0ee71697307a1'>HuffPost UK</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.theguardian.com/politics/2026/may/17/culture-secretary-lisa-nandy-says-wes-streeting-rejoin-eu-call-odd'>Top of Labour in row over EU as Nandy challenges Streeting’s remarks</a> - theguardian.com</li><li><a href='https://www.ft.com/content/6338ad6f-cf26-44c6-96e4-c64f1db615dd'>Burnham plays down rejoining EU after Streeting advocates Brexit reversal</a> - ft.com</li><li><a href='https://www.dawn.com/news/2000862/right-leaning-streeting-set-to-mount-challenge-for-labour-le